# correlation_boundary_analysis.py
##############################################################################

======================================================================================
R13. THE VARIANCE INFLATION THE INDEPENDENT BOUNDARY IGNORES
======================================================================================
  AR(1) within blocks of k = 3; blocks independent, so the factor does
  not depend on how many blocks an arm contains.

     rho   SD inflation   crit, independent   crit, adjusted
  ------------------------------------------------------------
     0.0         1.0000               24.2%            24.2%
     0.2         1.1372               24.2%            26.2%
     0.3         1.2083               24.2%            27.2%
     0.4         1.2806               24.2%            28.2%
     0.5         1.3540               24.2%            29.3%

======================================================================================
R13. TWO DIFFERENT QUANTITIES, STATED SEPARATELY
======================================================================================
  Independent boundary throughout; only the true rho changes.

   true rho     boundary type I error   classification specificity
               (tau = margin exactly)       (all tau below margin)
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
        0.0               4.92% ± 0.07                       99.3%
        0.2               7.67% ± 0.09                       98.6%
        0.3               9.04% ± 0.09                       97.9%
        0.4              10.49% ± 0.10                       97.3%
        0.5              11.81% ± 0.10                       96.7%

  Specificity moves by a few points while the error rate at the boundary
  more than doubles. Reporting only the first hides the failure.

======================================================================================
R13. THREE BOUNDARY STRATEGIES
======================================================================================
  matched      : the boundary uses the true rho (an upper bound on what
                 a well-informed trial could do)
  independent  : the boundary assumes rho = 0 (the previous version)
  conservative : the boundary always assumes rho = 0.5,
                 which is what a trial without a correlation estimate
                 can prespecify

   true rho |      independent       |        matched         |      conservative     
            |     error         sens |     error         sens |     error         sens
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        0.0 |     4.92%        82.0% |     4.92%        82.0% |     1.06%        74.4%
        0.2 |     7.67%        81.9% |     4.91%        79.0% |     2.20%        74.4%
        0.3 |     9.04%        81.8% |     4.84%        77.5% |     2.97%        74.3%
        0.4 |    10.49%        81.7% |     4.76%        75.8% |     3.79%        74.4%
        0.5 |    11.81%        81.4% |     4.69%        74.2% |     4.69%        74.2%

======================================================================================
SUMMARY
======================================================================================
  Worst-case type I error across rho 0 to 0.5:
    independent boundary  11.81%
    matched boundary      4.92%
    conservative boundary 4.69%

  Cost of the conservative boundary when errors are in fact
  independent: sensitivity 82.0% -> 74.4% (-7.6 points).

  A trial that cannot estimate the within-period correlation in advance should
  prespecify the conservative boundary and accept that loss, or plan an interim
  recalibration once enough within-period pairs have been observed to estimate
  rho. Reporting a 5% overall type I error without naming the independent-error
  reference model overstates what the procedure guarantees.

  Reproduce with: python3 validation/correlation_boundary_analysis.py

##############################################################################
# protocol_dependence_analysis.py
##############################################################################

========================================================================================
R15. ONSET RAMP: THE ASSUMPTION THAT THE EFFECT IS PRESENT AT DAY ONE
========================================================================================
  CV = 0.264, primary rule, mean of 30 replications.
  Measurements are taken in weeks 2, 3 and 4 of each 4-week period.

  onset time constant             sens    spec    weak
  ----------------------------------------------------
  none (effect immediate)        65.1%   98.7%     12%
  0.5 weeks                      64.5%   98.5%     12%
  1 week                         60.2%   98.7%      9%
  2 weeks                        45.6%   99.0%      7%
  3 weeks                        31.5%   99.2%      5%

  This is the single most consequential assumption in the protocol. A 2-week
  onset constant costs 19.5 sensitivity points, more than three times what
  moving from the intermediate to the pessimistic variance split costs, and a
  3-week constant costs 33.6. The design mitigates onset only by measuring late
  in the period, which is why the draws sit in weeks 2 to 4; with a 4-week
  period there is no room to wait longer. A trial that cannot bound the onset
  rate cannot bound its own sensitivity.

========================================================================================
R15. WASHOUT LENGTH AND CARRYOVER
========================================================================================
  Washout changes the schedule; with no time trend the ratio estimator is
  unaffected by timing, so washout acts on performance only through
  carryover and through total duration.

    washout  carryover    sens    spec   weeks
  ----------------------------------------------
        2wk        0%   65.1%   98.7%    29.3
        2wk       10%   62.1%   98.9%    29.4
        2wk       20%   58.5%   98.8%    29.6
        2wk       40%   50.5%   99.0%    29.9
        3wk        0%   65.1%   98.7%    34.2
        3wk       10%   62.1%   98.9%    34.3
        3wk       20%   58.5%   98.8%    34.5
        3wk       40%   50.5%   99.0%    34.9
        4wk        0%   65.1%   98.7%    39.1
        4wk       10%   62.1%   98.9%    39.2
        4wk       20%   58.5%   98.8%    39.5
        4wk       40%   50.5%   99.0%    39.9

  Carryover costs sensitivity at every washout length; a longer washout
  buys calendar time, not performance, unless it also reduces carryover.
  The trial that can measure the reversion rate is the one that can
  choose the washout; we cannot choose it from these simulations.

========================================================================================
R15. PARTIAL ADHERENCE
========================================================================================
  scenario                                    sens    spec
  --------------------------------------------------------
  full adherence                             65.1%   98.7%
  20% of patients at 75% adherence           61.3%   98.7%
  20% of patients at 50% adherence           58.1%   98.4%
  30% of patients at 50% adherence           53.6%   98.5%
  30% of patients at 25% adherence           58.3%   98.7%

  Adherence enters as a reduction in the delivered effect, so a patient
  who does not take the intervention is a simulated non-responder under
  the estimand. Whether a trial should treat them that way or as a
  protocol deviation is a design decision, not a simulation result.

========================================================================================
R15. WORST PLAUSIBLE COMBINATION
========================================================================================
  reference at CV 0.264                       65.1% sens    98.7% spec   12% weak
  onset 2 weeks + 20% carryover                38.0% sens    99.0% spec    5% weak

  Sensitivity falls 27.0 points and weak-responder detection
  falls from 12% to 5%. The operational parameters are not a
  footnote to the design: at the reachable CV they move performance more
  than the measurement precision this paper is about. Any recommendation
  about period length, washout or onset that these simulations do not
  support has been removed from the manuscript rather than softened.

  Reproduce with: python3 validation/protocol_dependence_analysis.py

